    "The final stage of this behavioral analysis involves the synthesis of a high-frequency **Sentiment Index**. As established by **Renault (2017)**, the core value of behavioral alternative data lies in its capacity to quantify \"Information Arrival\" and \"Crowd Expectation\" in real-time, effectively mitigating informational asymmetries that traditional reporting cannot address. By mapping qualitative user-generated labels to a discretized numerical scale, we transform high-entropy, heuristic-driven market chatter into a continuous **\"Optimism Proxy.\"**\n",
    "\n",
    "Consistent with standard methodologies in financial time-series analysis for managing the signal-to-noise ratio in retail-driven data, we apply a **7-day rolling mean** to the aggregate daily sentiment. This low-pass filtering technique is essential for isolating low-frequency behavioral signals from high-frequency stochastic noise (**Antweiler & Frank, 2004**). The resulting index allows for the identification of sustained regimes of market sentiment—either bullish or bearish—providing a nontraditional predictive feature that can be integrated into the Multilayer Perceptron (MLP) forecasting models utilized in this study."
